.TH su "" "Command"
.SH NAME
su \- substitute user id, become superuser
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsu [\fIuser \fB[\fIcommand \fB[\fIarg ...\fB]]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
su -- substitute user id, become superuser
Usage:	su [ user [ command ]]
Default 'user' is 'root'; default 'command' is 'sh'.
.HE
.B su
looks up
.I user
(default
.BR root ,
user number 0) in the password file.
If that account has a password,
.B su
prompts for a password and compares it, encrypted, against the
account's password; if they do not match,
.B su
prints
.B Sorry
and exits.
.PP
.B su
then sets the group id and user id of the current process to those of
.IR user ,
and executes
.I command
with the remaining arguments (default
.BR sh ,
with no arguments), searching
.B PATH
for it as
.B execvp
does.
Before doing so,
.B su
sets the environment variable
.B PS1
(the shell prompt) to
.B "# "
if
.I user
is the superuser, and to
.B "$ "
otherwise, so that a shell started this way is easy to recognize.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/passwd\fR	login names, user numbers, and passwords
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B su
prints
.B Sorry
and exits with a nonzero status if the password does not match.
It prints ``\fIn\fR: bad user number'' (if
.I user
was given as a number
.IR n )
or ``\fIuser\fR: not a user name'', and exits, if
.I user
is not found in the password file.
It prints ``\fIcommand\fR: not found'' if
.I command
cannot be executed.
.SH NOTES
.B su
does not change the current directory; the new command starts in
whatever directory was current when
.B su
was invoked.
